import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

// Invariant: value >= 0.
public class Counter {
    private int value;
    private final List<String> history = new ArrayList<>();

    public Counter(int start) {
        if (start < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("start must be >= 0");
        this.value = start;
    }

    public void increment() {
        value++;
        history.add("inc -> " + value);
    }

    public void reset() { value = 0; history.add("reset"); }

    public int value() { return value; }                  // read-only view.

    public List<String> history() { return List.copyOf(history); }   // not the internal list.

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Counter c = new Counter(2);
        c.increment();
        System.out.println(c.value() + " " + c.history());   // 3 [inc -> 3]
    }
}
```

| Design choice | Why |
|---|---|
| private fields. | only the class changes them. |
| validating constructor. | no invalid object is ever created. |
| operations, not setters. | each keeps the rule. |
| copies of internals. | callers can't mutate hidden state. |

